-- MAGIC     <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px;">
-- MAGIC         <span style="font-size: 24px;">ℹ️</span>
-- MAGIC         <div>
-- MAGIC             <strong style="color: #0d47a1; font-size: 1.1em;">Key Considerations</strong>
-- MAGIC             <ul>
-- MAGIC               <li>Enables phased adoption and thorough validation before legacy HMS decommission.</li>
-- MAGIC               <li>Reduced operational risk; easier to rollback if issues are detected.</li>
-- MAGIC               <li>More effort required to synchronize changes between environments during the migration.</li>
-- MAGIC               <li>Best for complex environments, organizations requiring zero downtime, or those with heavy ML/Declarative Pipeline dependencies.</li>
-- MAGIC             </ul>
-- MAGIC         </div>
-- MAGIC     </div>
-- MAGIC </div>
-- MAGIC

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC ### B1. Overall Process
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Spin up a new workspace attached to UC.
-- MAGIC 1. Migrate identities, permissions, storage locations, tables, and views using UCX or similar toolsets.
-- MAGIC 1. Refactor workloads and pipelines sequentially, moving user groups one at a time.
-- MAGIC 1. Validate each phase and cutover when ready.

-- COMMAND ----------

-- MAGIC %md-sandbox
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ## C. HMS Federation
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC HMS federation isn't necessarily a migration approach; rather it's a feature that reduces friction and pain during the migration by establishing a federated connection to an existing HMS, exposing HMS tables as foreign catalogs inside UC. This offers immediate UC governance and lineage features without moving data or rewriting workloads.
